[# Section 6 — the CX code language.
   Authored / expanded 2026-05-22.
   Anchored in spec/03-approved/core/code.md (§5.5 CXPath),
   spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md §4.6 (EBV).
   Largest section in the guide. Cross-links use [[anchor]] form.
   File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=code group='Ring 1 — Code' n=6 title='The code language'

  [child id=overview n=6.1 title='The CX code language'
    [intro
      """CX code is CX data with a leading `?` on the element name. A
         directive like `[?for]` is a normal element to the parser; the
         evaluator recognizes the `?` prefix and dispatches on the
         directive name. Everything else — patterns, paths, function
         calls, builtins, errors — is built from ordinary CX shapes."""]

    [body
      """Data is data; code is data plus a `?`:"""]
    [fixture id=program-for-g6-01-code-is-data-plus-a-question-mark]

    [body
      """The evaluator is `cx_code_eval(input, program, output_target,
         err_out)` at the C ABI level; every binding's Layer-1
         `Doc.eval(code)` wraps it (see [[bindings]]). The full
         normative spec is
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md).
         This section is the working developer guide."""]

    [body
      """Code can do five things: **select** (CXPath, see [[cxpath]]),
         **iterate** (`[?for]`, see [[for]]), **dispatch**
         (`[?match]`, see [[match]]), **bind** (`[?let]` / `[?def]` /
         `[?fn]`), and **update** (`[?modify]`, see [[modify]]). Every
         other directive is one of these five lifted into a specific
         domain — concurrency, async, resilience, services."""]

    [body
      """**Code is data.** A directive is an element whose name happens
         to start with `?`. You can pattern-match on `[?for ...]`
         exactly the way you pattern-match on `[user ...]`. You can
         transform code with `[?modify]`. You can hash it, project it
         to JSON, ship it across a wire, and evaluate it on the other
         end. The render rules in [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         §10.1 turn any well-formed program into a sequence diagram
         deterministically — because the program is just a tree."""]

    [body
      """**Pure-functional core.** Every directive returns a value;
         there is no assignment, no mutation, no rebinding. The
         concurrency primitives (channels, workers, async) exchange
         only immutable values, so there is no shared-mutable-state
         hazard by construction. See [[pure-updates]] for the
         lens/zipper foundation that `[?modify]` builds on."""]]

  [child id=directives n=6.2 title='Directive registry'
    [intro
      """There are currently **eighty normative directives**.
         Adding or removing any directive requires a governance change per
         [`spec/process/governance.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/process/governance.md). The
         authoritative registry lives in
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §4.1; this
         subsection is an at-a-glance grouping of the directives this
         guide teaches — see the registry for the complete set."""]

    [body
      """**Core constructs (11).** Built into the evaluator;
         everything else composes from these:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'directive'] [th 'purpose']]
      [tr [td '[?match]'] [td 'Pattern dispatch — single-arm assertion or multi-arm first-match (see [[match]])']]
      [tr [td '[?modify]'] [td 'Pure-functional update — apply an action at every CXPath focus (see [[modify]])']]
      [tr [td '[?for]'] [td 'Iteration / comprehension over a CXPath, pattern, or sequence (see [[for]])']]
      [tr [td '[?let]'] [td 'Local binding scoped to a body (see [[let]])']]
      [tr [td '[?fn]'] [td 'Anonymous function literal — body of $bindings → expression']]
      [tr [td '[?def]'] [td 'Named top-level definition (function or value)']]
      [tr [td '[?if]'] [td 'Boolean branch with EBV condition (see [[if]] + [[ebv]])']]
      [tr [td '[?else]'] [td 'Value-or-default coalesce — recover on [err] or absence (getOrElse; see [[errors]])']]
      [tr [td '[?pipe]'] [td 'Pipeline — canonical form of the `|` operator (see [[pipe]])']]
      [tr [td '[?map]'] [td 'Map a closure over a sequence (sequential by default; the bareword [par] clause enables parallel evaluation and always preserves source order; [ordered] is a tombstoned no-op)']]
      [tr [td '[?reduce]'] [td 'Fold a sequence to a single value via a binary closure starting from [init …] (sequential left-fold by default; [par] requires associativity)']]]

    [body
      """**Resilience (6).** Wrap an inner expression with a
         failure-handling policy. See [[resilience]] for the parameter
         tables:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'directive'] [th 'purpose']]
      [tr [td '[?retry]'] [td 'Re-run on failure with backoff + jitter; bounded by max=']]
      [tr [td '[?timeout]'] [td 'Bound execution time; cancel + return [on-timeout …] on expiry']]
      [tr [td '[?circuit-breaker]'] [td 'Open after threshold-exceeded failures in a rolling window']]
      [tr [td '[?fallback]'] [td 'Run [recover-with …] secondary if primary returns [err …] ($err bound in the recovery)']]
      [tr [td '[?rate-limit]'] [td 'Token-bucket cap — max= calls per per= duration']]
      [tr [td '[?bulkhead]'] [td 'Bound max-concurrent= in-flight; optional [queue …]']]]

    [body
      """**Services + clients (3).** HTTP service definition + client
         construction. See [[services]]:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'directive'] [th 'purpose']]
      [tr [td '[?http-service]'] [td 'Declare an HTTP service with resource handlers; returns a handle']]
      [tr [td '[?service-handle]'] [td 'Look up an existing service by name=']]
      [tr [td '[?http-client]'] [td 'Construct an HTTP client (optionally wrapped in resilience)']]]

    [body
      """**Concurrency (11).** Workers + channels + subscriptions +
         selection. See [[workers]]:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'directive'] [th 'purpose']]
      [tr [td '[?worker]'] [td 'Spawn a worker; concurrent with siblings in the enclosing scope']]
      [tr [td '[?worker-handle]'] [td 'Look up an existing worker by name=']]
      [tr [td '[?channel]'] [td 'Create a FIFO channel — [?channel name=… buffer=N]; buffer=0 is rendezvous']]
      [tr [td '[?subscribe]'] [td 'Attach a subscription to a retained channel — resume from a position within the retained window']]
      [tr [td '[?monitor]'] [td 'Subscribe to a worker lifecycle-event stream (spawned / cancelled / panicked / done)']]
      [tr [td '[?send]'] [td 'Blocking send to a channel; returns [ok] or CHANNEL_CLOSED']]
      [tr [td '[?try-send]'] [td 'Send with timeout= — SEND_TIMEOUT if buffer stays full']]
      [tr [td '[?receive]'] [td 'Blocking receive — returns next value or CHANNEL_CLOSED']]
      [tr [td '[?try-receive]'] [td 'Receive with timeout= — RECV_TIMEOUT if empty']]
      [tr [td '[?close]'] [td 'Close a channel; second close raises CHANNEL_ALREADY_CLOSED']]
      [tr [td '[?select]'] [td 'Multi-way receive — first ready case wins (uniform-random on ties)']]]

    [body
      """**Async (8).** Futures + barriers + cancellation. See
         [[async]]:"""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'directive'] [th 'purpose']]
      [tr [td '[?async]'] [td 'Evaluate EXPR asynchronously; return a future immediately']]
      [tr [td '[?await]'] [td 'Block until a future is terminal; return value or [err]']]
      [tr [td '[?await-all]'] [td 'Join N futures — all-or-error barrier']]
      [tr [td '[?await-any]'] [td 'Yield first to terminate; others continue running']]
      [tr [td '[?await-race]'] [td 'Yield first to terminate; cancel the rest']]
      [tr [td '[?cancel]'] [td 'Request cooperative cancellation on a future/worker handle']]
      [tr [td '[?check-cancel]'] [td 'Polling cancellation point inside a CPU-bound loop']]
      [tr [td '[?sleep]'] [td 'Cancellable sleep for DURATION']]]

    [body
      """**Lifecycle (2).** `[?stop]` — initiate graceful shutdown on a
         service or worker handle — and `[?wait-for]` — block until a
         service or worker terminates, returning its terminal value or
         `[err]`."""]

    [note
      """**Retired in v0.8.0.** `[?find]` is no longer a directive. Use [; version-literal-ok ]
         `[?for]` with a pattern generator, or CXPath `//pattern`. See
         [[migration]] for mechanical migration rules. The rationale:
         CXPath is the universal selector, so the
         `[?find]` shape was redundant with `[?for]`+CXPath."""]

    [note
      """**Implementation status.** Multi-arm `[?match]`,
         `[?modify]`, and the CXPath value kind in
         constructs other than `[?for]` are **fully
         specified** but not all are wired through every binding
         yet. The reference evaluator carries the spec-conformant
         implementation; the bindings follow per the parity matrix in
         [`spec/misc/parity-matrix.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md). Where
         a feature is spec'd-but-not-shipped this guide says so
         explicitly."""]]

  [child id=cxpath n=6.3 title='CXPath — the universal selector'
    [intro
      """CXPath is the single selector vocabulary across reading,
         iteration, dispatch, and update. It aligns with XPath 3.1
         (see [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5.5)
         with deliberate divergences kept minimal. **Path is a value
         kind**, not parser sugar — paths compile, cache, round-trip
         through every projection, and visualize as themselves."""]

    [body
      """The simplest CXPath is the descendant search:"""]

    [body
      """`//user` is every user anywhere in the document;
         `//user[= $_@active true]` every active one; `//user/@email` the
         attribute values; `//section[1]` the first section (1-indexed);
         `//section[h2]` the sections that have an `h2` child:"""]
    [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-02-descendant-search-basics]

    [body
      """The grammar is the XPath 3.1 **selection core**, lifted into
         CX with one comparison operator family:"""]

    [example lang=text
      """PathExpr   ::= '//' StepList | '/' StepList | StepList
         Step       ::= ( Axis '::' )? NodeTest Predicate*
         Predicate  ::= '[' PredExpr ']'
         PredExpr   ::= INT | AttrTest | StepTest | ScalarLiteral
                      | BindingOrPath | FusedPrefixForm"""]

    [child id=cxpath-axes n=6.3.1 title='The twelve axes'
      [body
        """CXPath supports twelve axes. The default axis is `child`,
           so `name` ≡ `child::name`. The full set:"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'axis'] [th 'direction'] [th 'what-it-selects']]
        [tr [td 'child'] [td 'down'] [td 'Direct children of context node (default axis)']]
        [tr [td 'descendant'] [td 'down'] [td 'Children, grandchildren, deeper — excludes self']]
        [tr [td 'descendant-or-self'] [td 'down'] [td '`descendant` plus the context node itself']]
        [tr [td 'parent'] [td 'up'] [td 'The unique parent of context (or empty at root)']]
        [tr [td 'ancestor'] [td 'up'] [td 'Parent, grandparent, up to root — excludes self']]
        [tr [td 'ancestor-or-self'] [td 'up'] [td '`ancestor` plus the context node itself']]
        [tr [td 'following-sibling'] [td 'lateral'] [td 'Later siblings with same parent']]
        [tr [td 'preceding-sibling'] [td 'lateral'] [td 'Earlier siblings with same parent']]
        [tr [td 'following'] [td 'document'] [td 'Every node after context in document order (excluding descendants)']]
        [tr [td 'preceding'] [td 'document'] [td 'Every node before context in document order (excluding ancestors)']]
        [tr [td 'self'] [td 'self'] [td 'Context node itself — useful for predicate filters']]
        [tr [td 'attribute'] [td 'attr'] [td 'Attributes of context — shorthand is `@name`']]]
      [body
        """The common idioms, one per axis — all descendants of a
           section, the section enclosing a code block, the users after
           the first, the table around a row, the sibling just before a
           code block, an attribute filter, and the attribute axis spelled
           in full (`//user/attribute::email` is `//user/@email`):"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-03-axis-idioms]
      [body
        """The same step vocabulary applies from a **binding**
           (grammar [135a]): `$u/name` (child), `$u//item`
           (descendant), `$u@email` (attribute), `$u/..` (parent),
           and `$m.key` (map-key access on a map-valued binding).
           See [[bindings]]."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-predicates n=6.3.2 title='Predicates — positional, attribute, function, composite'
      [body
        """A predicate is `[...]` after a step. The predicate body is
           **prefix CX code over the context node** `$_` — plus a
           small set of atomic shorthands. The admitted shapes:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Positional** — `INT` selects the Nth match
                (1-indexed). `//section[1]` is the first section.
                Negative positions are NOT supported; `[$_last]`
                selects the last, `[= $_position N]` the Nth."""]
        [item """**Attribute test** — `[@name]` (attribute present)
                and `[@!name]` (attribute absent). Value comparisons
                are prefix forms: `[= $_@name V]`, `[!= …]`,
                `[< …]` etc."""]
        [item """**Step-existence** — a bare child name: `[h2]`
                keeps elements that have an `h2` child."""]
        [item """**Prefix expression** — any prefix CX form over
                `$_`: comparisons (`[= $_@id 42]`), boolean
                combinators (`[and …]` / `[or …]` / `[not …]`), and
                function calls (`[$strings:contains $_@email
                '@example.com']`)."""]]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-04-predicate-shapes]
      [body
        """Predicates chain: `//user[= $_@active true][1]` is the
           first active user — the second predicate filters the
           result of the first. The order matters."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-comparison n=6.3.3 title='Comparison — one operator family'
      [body
        """**One operator syntax: `= != < <= > >=`.** No keyword
           synonyms (`eq` / `ne` / `lt` / ...) — they are not in the
           grammar. Semantics are **value-comparison**: both sides
           must be single values; a multi-valued operand raises
           `CXER0103`; no type coercion."""]
      [body
        """This is the deliberate divergence from XPath 3.1's
           general-comparison default. In XPath, `//x = 1` is true if
           *any* `x` equals 1 — a common gotcha when the path
           silently returns multiple nodes. In CX, the path must
           return a single value, or the comparison raises. To
           express "any equals", filter the sequence itself:
           `[$exists //user/tag[= [$text $_] 'admin']]`."""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-05-value-comparison]]

    [child id=cxpath-bindings n=6.3.4 title='Paths on bindings'
      [body
        """A CXPath rooted at a binding navigates from that value
           instead of from the document root:"""]
      [body
        """`$u/name` is the direct child, `$u/*` all children, `$u//item`
           the descendants, `$u@active` the attribute shorthand and
           `$u/@active` the full axis form:"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-06-paths-on-bindings]
      [body
        """The path operators that work on bindings are the same
           operators that work on the implicit document — one
           vocabulary. The leading `//` form is anchored at the
           **document root**, regardless of context; `$u//x` is
           anchored at `$u`. Use `//x` to escape upward when you
           already have a binding. Parent and map-key steps ride the
           same surface (grammar [135a]): `$u/..` steps to the
           parent, and `$m.key` reads a key from a map-valued
           binding."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-context n=6.3.5 title='Where CXPath values are accepted'
      [body
        """CXPath is a value kind. Every construct that takes "a
           selector" accepts a CXPath value:"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-07-path-in-every-position]
      [body
        """A path appears as a `[?for]` generator, as an `[?if]`
           condition, as a `[?match]` arm, as a `[?let]` initializer, as
           a `[?modify]` focus, and as a builtin argument — the six
           positions above, in that order. This is the unification we
           ratify: no separate "path syntax" per construct."""]
      [body
        """**Status note.** CXPath value-kind at non-`[?for]`
           positions is production-ready in V; full Tier-2 binding parity is
           tracked in
           [`spec/misc/parity-matrix.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-divergences n=6.3.6 title='Deliberate divergences from XPath 3.1'
      [body
        """CXPath is a working subset of XPath 3.1 + deliberate
           changes. The normative treatment is
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5.5;
           the headline differences:"""]
      [list
        [item """**One comparison family** (`= != < <= > >=`) —
                XPath's split into general (`=`) and value (`eq`) is
                gone; CX uses value-comparison semantics
                exclusively. Rationale: the general-comparison
                gotcha is XPath's most-reported source of subtle
                bugs."""]
        [item """**Path is a value kind** — XPath paths are syntactic
                fragments inside XPath expressions only. In CX they
                are first-class values."""]
        [item """**1-indexed positions** — adopted from XPath
                verbatim (`//x[1]` is the first), but CX explicitly
                rejects `[0]` (raises `CXER0100`); XPath silently
                yields the empty sequence."""]
        [item """**No general-purpose** `for` / `let` **inside paths** —
                CX moves binding into the `[?for]` / `[?let]`
                directive surface; predicates take expressions but
                not binding clauses. Use the surrounding directive."""]
        [item """**No paren-calls anywhere** — XPath's `count(//user)`
                spelling does not exist; every call is the
                head-dispatch form `[$count //user]`, in predicates
                too (`[> [$count $_/*] 0]`). `position()` / `last()`
                are the reserved predicate bindings `$_position` /
                `$_last`."""]]
      [note
        """The normative CXPath treatment — semantics, the desugar
           table, and predicate evaluation — is
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5.5;
           the XPath 3.1 parity corpus is
           [`conformance/xpath_31_parity.cxd`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/conformance/xpath_31_parity.cxd)."""]]]

  [child id=patterns n=6.4 title='Patterns and matching'
    [intro
      """A **pattern** is a literal CX subtree extended with binding
         holes (`$name`), wildcards (`*`, `**`, `_`), type guards
         (`:TypeName`), scalar literals, and CXPath values. Patterns
         appear in `[?for]`'s first generator, in `[?match]` arms,
         and in destructuring `[?let]`. The full pattern grammar is
         in [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5."""]

    [child id=pattern-element-shape n=6.4.1 title='Element-shape patterns'
      [body
        """An element-shape pattern matches an element by name,
           attributes, and (recursively) body content. The pattern
           uses ordinary CX syntax with bind-holes where you want
           to capture values:"""]
      [body
        """`[user email=$e]` binds the email attribute; `[user @active=true
           $u]` filters on an attribute and binds the whole user;
           `[user @id=42 [name $n]]` binds a child's body; `[post
           author=$a body=$b]` binds two attributes:"""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-08-element-shape-patterns]
      [body
        """**Binding semantics depend on context.** A pattern
           `[NAME $x]` **without** attribute predicates auto-unwraps
           the body — text body binds the string, scalar body binds
           the scalar, empty body binds the element itself,
           structured body binds the element. A pattern
           `[NAME @attr… $x]` **with** attribute predicates always
           binds `$x` to the matched element. The rationale is in
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5.2 rule 5: when
           you name attributes you are filtering "what kind of
           element", so the bind value is the element; when you
           don't, the bind value is the inner content."""]]

    [child id=pattern-scalar n=6.4.2 title='Scalar literal patterns'
      [body
        """Scalar literals are valid patterns. They match by
           value-equality:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-g6-09-scalar-literal-arms]
      [body
        """Integers, floats, strings, bools, dates, datetimes, and
           `null` are all matchable as scalar literals. Float
           equality follows IEEE-754 — `nan` is **never** equal
           to `nan`. Test for NaN explicitly with
           `[?lib 'cx-stdlib/math']` and `[$math:is-nan $x]`."""]]

    [child id=pattern-cxpath n=6.4.3 title='CXPath patterns'
      [body
        """A CXPath value used as a pattern matches if the
           candidate value is in the path's result sequence:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-g6-10-cxpath-arms]
      [body
        """In a `[?for]` body, the same CXPath as the generator
           selects directly:"""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-11-cxpath-generator-selects]
      [body
        """CXPath patterns and element-shape patterns compose in
           the same arm list — `[?match]` can dispatch on a mix of
           both, with first-match-wins ordering (see [[match]])."""]]

    [child id=pattern-wildcards n=6.4.4 title='Wildcards — *, **, _'
      [body
        """Three wildcard forms:"""]
      [list
        [item """`*` — **any single element.** `[* $x]` matches any
                element and binds it. `[section * $body]` matches
                any section and binds the body wildcard slot."""]
        [item """`**` — **recursive descent.** `[**]` matches any
                element at this position or deeper. Wrapping an
                inner pattern — `[** [user $u]]` — matches a `user`
                anywhere in the body and binds it."""]
        [item """`_` — **anything, no bind.** `_` matches any value
                (including non-elements) without binding it. The
                idiomatic catch-all in `[?match]`."""]]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-g6-12-wildcards]]

    [child id=pattern-binding n=6.4.5 title='Named bindings — $name'
      [body
        """`$name` (lowercase, leading `$`) is a binding hole. It
           appears in attribute-value position
           (`[user email=$e]`), in body position (`[user $u]`),
           and in a destructuring `[?for]` generator
           (`[?for [in [user $u] /users/user] …]`)."""]
      [body
        """A bind hole always succeeds at its position; the
           bind value depends on what the surrounding pattern
           dictates (see [[pattern-element-shape]]). Naming
           collisions inside one arm raise `CXER0100`
           (PARSE_ERROR) — `[user @id=$x @name=$x]` would require
           the values be equal, which the current spec does not
           support; use a single bind and check with a `[when …]` clause."""]]

    [child id=pattern-type-guards n=6.4.6 title='Type-guard heads'
      [body
        """A type guard `:TypeName` at **pattern-head position**
           narrows by schema-typed shape:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-g6-13-type-guard-without-schema]
      [body
        """Type-guard resolution is by the document's schema
           binding (see [[schema]] in the data section); with no
           schema in scope a type guard is refused at program load,
           as the fixture above shows (`CXER0100`, naming the type and
           the three ways to bring a schema into scope). Pair with
           schema binding (`[?cx schema='…']`) for type-driven
           dispatch."""]]]

  [child id=bindings n=6.5 title='Bindings and paths'
    [intro
      """A binding is a name introduced by `[?let]`, `[?for]`,
         `[?fn]`, or `[?match]` pattern. Bindings are referenced via
         `$name`. They are immutable — there is no rebinding (per
         CX's pure-functional discipline)."""]

    [fixture id=program-let-g6-14-binding-then-iterate]

    [body
      """A binding's value is whatever expression produced it:
         a sequence, a scalar, an element, an array, a map, an
         error, a future, a CXPath, a function literal. Every value
         is a sequence (per the sequence-flat principle); single
         values are sequences of one."""]

    [body
      """**Navigation from a binding** uses CXPath syntax (see
         [[cxpath-bindings]]). The path operators that work on
         bindings are the same operators that work on the implicit
         document — one vocabulary. `$u/email` is a child step;
         `$u@email` is the attribute shorthand; `$u/@email` is the
         full attribute axis form."""]

    [body
      """**Scope.** A binding is in scope inside the directive
         body that introduced it. `[?let [= $x E] BODY]` puts
         `$x` in scope for `BODY` only; outside the let it does
         not exist. `[?for [in $u SEQ] [yield E]]` puts `$u` in
         scope for `E` (one iteration at a time, per item)."""]

    [body
      """**Shadowing.** A nested binding with the same name shadows
         the outer for the inner scope; this is the standard lexical
         rule and matches every host language. CX does not warn on
         shadowing — readability is the author's responsibility."""]]

  [child id=constructs n=6.6 title='Core constructs'
    [intro
      """The six core constructs are how every CX program is built.
         Each is documented in the following sub-sections."""]

    [child id=for n=6.6.1 title='[?for] — iteration'
      [body
        """`[?for]` is the iteration / comprehension primitive. The
           expression in the `[in …]` clause is either a CXPath
           (yields a sequence to iterate), a pattern (matches the
           generator — the `[?find]` replacement), or any expression
           that evaluates to a sequence."""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-15-six-generator-forms]
      [body
        """`[par]` and `[lazy]` are evaluation-modifier clauses —
           same surface, different scheduler. `[par]` distributes
           iterations across a bounded worker pool (`min(4, ncpu)`
           workers by default; `[par N]` caps it at N) and **always
           reassembles results in source order** — the retired
           `[ordered]` clause survives as a tombstoned no-op. `[lazy]`
           couples the generator to a streaming input source so that
           the iteration runs in bounded memory regardless of input
           size. Full semantics in
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §7."""]
      [body
        """**Multiple `[where …]` clauses** AND together; **multiple
           `[yield …]` clauses** concatenate (per the sequence-flat
           principle — yields never produce a list-of-lists)."""]]

    [child id=match n=6.6.2 title='[?match] — dispatch (single + multi-arm)'
      [body
        """`[?match]` is pattern dispatch. CX supports two
           forms: **2-arg destructure** (single arm, asserts the
           value matches or raises) and **multi-arm** (first-match-
           wins across N arms). The multi-arm form is a recent
           addition."""]

      [body
        """**Single-arm form** — pattern-bind a value and assert
           the shape:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-g6-16-single-arm-destructure]

      [body
        """**Multi-arm form** — first-match-wins across `[case …]` /
           `[when …]` / `[else …]` clauses. Arms may mix element-shape
           patterns, scalar literals, CXPath paths, wildcards,
           and predicate-only `[when …]` clauses:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-g6-17-three-dispatch-shapes]

      [body
        """**Arm semantics:**"""]
      [list
        [item """`[case PAT R]` — **match against pattern PAT.**
                Arms evaluate top-to-bottom; the first one whose
                pattern matches "wins". Later arms are not tried,
                even if they would also have matched."""]
        [item """`[when PRED R]` — **match iff predicate truthy.**
                No pattern; the predicate is evaluated with the
                scrutinee in scope as `$_` (and any earlier `[case
                …]` bindings still in scope only if the same arm)."""]
        [item """`[else R]` — **catch-all, always last.** Equivalent
                to `[case _ R]`. Multiple `[else …]` clauses raise
                `CXER0100`."""]
        [item """`[case PAT [where PRED] R]` — **guard on a case
                arm.** The pattern must match AND the predicate
                must be truthy."""]
        [item """**No implicit EBV in** `[case …]`. `[case …]` tests
                pattern equality / structural match; it does not
                apply EBV. `[when …]` uses EBV."""]]

      [body
        """**No-match behavior differs by form.** The 2-arg form
           raises `CXER0100`; the multi-arm form yields `()` (the
           empty sequence) if no arm matches and no `[else …]` is
           present. An unreachable arm (covered by an earlier arm
           with no guard) raises a warning at parse time but does
           not fail compilation."""]

      [note
        """**Status.** Multi-arm `[?match]` is fully spec'd.
           V's evaluator carries the production-ready
           implementation; Tier-2 binding parity is per
           [`spec/misc/parity-matrix.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]

    [child id=if n=6.6.3 title='[?if] — boolean branch (with EBV)'
      [body
        """`[?if cond [then E1] [else E2]]` is the explicit boolean
           branch. The condition is evaluated with the EBV
           (Effective Boolean Value) rule — see [[ebv]] below."""]
      [fixture id=program-if-g6-18-ebv-conditions]
      [body
        """For multi-way conditionals prefer `[?match]` multi-arm
           (see [[match]]). `[?if]` stays separate from `[?match]`
           because they have different contracts — `[?if]` uses
           EBV (truthy/falsy with container rules), `[?match]`
           `[case …]` does **not** (it tests strictly by structural
           equality / pattern match)."""]
      [body
        """**The `[else …]` clause is optional.** When omitted, an
           untrue condition yields `()` (the empty sequence). Many
           CX idioms exploit this — `[?if $n@hidden [then ()] [else
           [$render $n]]]` reads as "render unless hidden"."""]

      [child id=ebv n=6.6.3.1 title='EBV — the Effective Boolean Value rule'
        [body
          """The EBV rule, applied in order (per
             [`spec/core/cxdm.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §4.6):"""]
        [list
          [item """**Empty sequence** `()` → false."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `bool` → that bool's
                  value."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `string` →
                  length > 0."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `int` / `float` →
                  value `!= 0` (and not `nan` for float)."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `null` → false."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `date` / `datetime` /
                  `bytes` → true (existence is truthy)."""]
          [item """**Single Node (element)** → true."""]
          [item """**Single Array** (v1.1 / v0.8.0) → true iff [; version-literal-ok ]
                  non-empty (length > 0)."""]
          [item """**Single Map** (v1.1) → true iff non-empty."""]
          [item """**Sequence of length > 1** → true."""]]
        [body
          """This is the rule that makes `[?if //service …]`
             mean "if any service exists", `[?if @debug …]` mean
             "if the debug attribute exists and is truthy", and
             `[?if @items …]` mean "if the items array is
             non-empty" when `@items` is an Array-typed
             attribute."""]
        [body
          """The container rules (Array/Map empty-is-falsy)
             follow the convention JSON-template engines and
             YAML/TOML processors share.
             CX deliberately diverges from XPath 3.1 here, which
             raises a type error on EBV(array); CX's pragmatic
             rule reads more naturally in template position."""]]]

    [child id=let n=6.6.4 title='[?let] — binding'
      [body
        """`[?let]` introduces a local binding. The bound name is
           in scope inside the body that follows."""]
      [fixture id=program-let-g6-19-sequential-bindings]
      [body
        """`[?let]` is the only way to give an expression a name
           currently — there is no `def` inside a function body,
           no inline `where` (that is a `[?for]` filter). To name
           a value that is used multiple times, `[?let]` is
           idiomatic."""]]

    [child id=modify n=6.6.5 title='[?modify] — pure-functional updates'
      [body
        """`[?modify]` is the update directive. It takes a
           document (or sub-tree), a CXPath focus, and one or
           more action slots; it returns a **new** document with
           the action applied at every focus match. The original
           is unchanged (pure-functional)."""]

      [body
        """The **eleven action slots** are:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'action'] [th 'description']]
        [tr [td '[set V]'] [td 'Replace matched value, element body, or attribute value with V']]
        [tr [td '[delete]'] [td 'Remove matched node from its parent body (or attribute from element)']]
        [tr [td '[using F]'] [td 'Apply function F to each match; F receives the match, returns the replacement']]
        [tr [td '[rename N]'] [td 'Rename matched element (preserves attrs + body)']]
        [tr [td '[set-attr N V]'] [td 'Add new attribute N=V, or overwrite if N already present']]
        [tr [td '[delete-attr N]'] [td 'Remove attribute N from matched element']]
        [tr [td '[append V]'] [td 'Add child V at end of matched body']]
        [tr [td '[prepend V]'] [td 'Add child V at start of matched body']]
        [tr [td '[insert-before V]'] [td 'Insert V as a sibling immediately before each match']]
        [tr [td '[insert-after V]'] [td 'Insert V as a sibling immediately after each match']]
        [tr [td '[replace V]'] [td 'Replace entire matched node (including its surrounding placement) with V']]]

      [body
        """Every action on one document, in the table's order — then a
           three-step pipeline (see [[pipe]]); each result is a new
           document, and the twelve results below share one unchanged
           original:"""]
      [fixture id=program-modify-g6-20-eleven-actions]

      [body
        """**Pure-functional — structural sharing.** `[?modify]`
           does not copy the whole tree. Only the spine from root
           to each matched node is copied; unchanged branches are
           shared with the original (immutable persistent data
           structure). The new allocation is O(depth × matches) —
           the spine, not the document size — and the
           original `$doc` value is still valid for read or hash."""]

      [body
        """**Multi-match.** The focus path selects a sequence; the
           action applies to every node in that sequence. Zero
           matches returns the original unchanged (not an error —
           the no-match-no-op invariant lets pipelines compose
           safely). All-matches-at-once means there is no
           ordering hazard between matches."""]

      [body
        """**Identity invariant.** `[?modify $doc //x [set v]]`
           where `//x` matches no nodes returns `$doc` unchanged
           — same canonical bytes, same SHA-256. `[?modify $doc
           //x [delete]]` where every `//x` is removed returns a
           value where `[$count //x]` is 0. Round-trip through
           ast-bin / data-bin / JSON preserves the post-modify
           shape exactly."""]

      [note
        """**Status.** `[?modify]` is fully spec'd.
           The V evaluator carries the production-ready
           implementation. Tier-2 binding parity is tracked in
           [`spec/misc/parity-matrix.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]

    [child id=pipe n=6.6.6 title='Pipelines and composition'
      [body
        """The `[?pipe]` directive threads the output of one
           expression into the input of the next. Pipelines
           compose `[?modify]`, `[?for]`, builtins, and
           user-defined functions into readable top-to-bottom
           data flow."""]
      [fixture id=program-pipe-g6-21-stages-are-transforms]
      [body
        """**Stages are bare transforms.** `[?pipe SRC STAGE …]`
           evaluates `SRC`, then supplies the value to each `STAGE`
           left-to-right. A stage is a transform — a bare name, a
           `$ref`, or a `[$call …]` — never wrapped in a keyword
           (there is no `[through]` clause). `[?pipe 5 add1 add1]`
           → `7`."""]
      [body
        """**Stage invocation (§8.9.1).** How the value is supplied
           depends on the stage shape: a **hole-form** stage carries
           a single partial-application hole `_` and the value fills
           it (`[$get _ '/users/42']` → `get(client, '/users/42')`);
           a **no-hole** stage receives the value **appended as its
           final positional argument** (`count` ≡ `[$count _]`). A
           pipe threads exactly one value, so a stage may carry **at
           most one** `_` (two or more → `cx-err:CXER0100`). A
           non-callable stage (a literal, map, or data element) is
           `cx-err:CXER0100` — it never silently passes the value
           through."""]
      [body
        """**Fan-out behavior.** When `SRC` evaluates to a sequence,
           a stage sees the whole sequence as a single value (the
           sequence-flat principle — no implicit per-item dispatch).
           For a per-item transform, thread a `[?fn]` lambda that
           runs the comprehension:"""]
      [fixture id=program-pipe-g6-22-fan-out-through-a-lambda]
      [body
        """`[tap f]` — **observe without diverting** (§8.9.2). A
           `[tap f]` stage applies `f` to the current value for its
           **side effect**; the value passes through unchanged and
           `f`'s return is discarded. Inside a stage, `_` names the
           current value, so `[tap [$log:info 'seen' fields={v: _}]]`
           logs and flows on. A tap propagates an `[err]` only when
           its code is `CXER0001` (panic) or `CXER0260`
           (cancellation); every other error is discarded for the
           data flow (the error-hook still observes it)."""]
      [body
        """**Railway short-circuit (§8.9.3).** The pipe is a
           single-channel failure railway: the first stage to yield
           `[err …]` skips every remaining stage — taps included —
           and that `[err]` is the result. An empty result is **not**
           a short-circuit; it flows to the next stage. There is no
           infix `|` and no `[through]` wrapper — both retired."""]]]

  [child id=builtins n=6.7 title='Built-in functions'
    [intro
      """Built-ins are the standard library at the expression
         layer. They are pure-functional, total over their
         declared input types, and stable across bindings.
         Full normative table in
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §6.5; the
         subsections below organize the ~47 names by category."""]

    [body
      """**Invocation.** Every call uses the
         bracket head-dispatch form: `[$name args…]` for the
         built-in surface, or `[$module:name args…]` for
         stdlib-module calls. There is **no paren-call form** —
         `name(args)` is a parse error everywhere. The
         whitespace-separated element form `[name a b]` without a
         leading `$` is **always** element construction; write
         `[$count //user]` (call) versus `[count //user]` (data
         element). The `name(args)` spelling in the tables below is
         arity notation (mirroring the spec), not invocation
         syntax."""]

    [child id=sequence-builtins n=6.7.1 title='Sequence built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on a Sequence. Argument may be a CX program
           sequence, the implicit document, or a single
           non-sequence value (treated as a 1-element
           sequence)."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'builtin'] [th 'arity'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'count(seq) / length(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Integer count; scalar input yields 1']]
        [tr [td 'empty(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Boolean — true iff count(seq) == 0']]
        [tr [td 'exists(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Boolean — true iff count(seq) > 0; opposite of empty']]
        [tr [td 'present(value)'] [td '1'] [td 'Boolean — true iff value is present; false only for absence (the empty sequence / empty node-set). The canonical "did this step match anything" test — unlike exists, it never looks inside: a childless element, an empty string, and null are all present']]
        [tr [td 'first(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'First item; scalar passes through; XQuery fn:head parity']]
        [tr [td 'last(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Last item; scalar passes through']]
        [tr [td 'head(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Synonym for first']]
        [tr [td 'tail(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'All items except first; scalar yields ()']]
        [tr [td 'reverse(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Items in reverse order; scalar passes through']]
        [tr [td 'distinct(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Items with structural duplicates removed; first-occurrence order']]
        [tr [td 'nth(seq, n)'] [td '2'] [td '1-indexed item access; out-of-range raises CXER0100']]
        [tr [td 'position(seq, item)'] [td '2'] [td '1-based index of first structural match; zero if absent']]
        [tr [td 'range(lo, hi, step?)'] [td '2-3'] [td 'Arithmetic progression, inclusive bound; [$range lo *] is a lazy open iterator']]
        [tr [td 'iterate(f, seed)'] [td '2'] [td 'Lazy infinite iterator: seed, f(seed), f(f(seed)), …']]
        [tr [td 'unfold(f, seed)'] [td '2'] [td 'Anamorphism: f(state) yields () to stop or [value, next-state] to emit']]
        [tr [td 'identity(x)'] [td '1'] [td 'Returns x unchanged — for pipe stages and shape testing']]]
      [body
        """Set combinators are **reserved operator heads**, not
           `[$…]` builtins: `[union A B]`, `[intersect A B]`,
           `[except A B]`. Slicing / windowing / ordering /
           grouping are directives and comprehension clauses:
           `[?take]`, `[?drop]`, `[?filter]`, `[?flatten]`,
           `[order-by …]`, `[group-by …]` (see [[for]])."""]
      [fixture id=program-builtin-g6-23-sequence-builtins]]

    [child id=string-builtins n=6.7.2 title='String built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on a String scalar. Non-string scalars are
           stringified by the canonical scalar printer; element
           arguments raise `CXER0100` unless documented
           otherwise."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'builtin'] [th 'arity'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'upper(s)'] [td '1'] [td 'Unicode upper-cased copy of s']]
        [tr [td 'lower(s)'] [td '1'] [td 'Unicode lower-cased copy of s']]
        [tr [td 'contains(s, sub)'] [td '2'] [td 'Boolean substring test; empty sub yields true']]
        [tr [td 'starts-with(s, prefix)'] [td '2'] [td 'Boolean prefix test; empty prefix yields true']]
        [tr [td 'ends-with(s, suffix)'] [td '2'] [td 'Boolean suffix test; empty suffix yields true']]
        [tr [td 'substring(s, start, len?)'] [td '2-3'] [td '1-indexed substring; positions clamp; never raises']]
        [tr [td 'string-length(s)'] [td '1'] [td 'Integer count of Unicode codepoints (NOT bytes)']]
        [tr [td 'normalize-space(s)'] [td '1'] [td 'Strip leading/trailing + collapse internal whitespace']]
        [tr [td 'concat(s1, s2, …)'] [td '≥1'] [td 'String concatenation; non-string scalars stringified']]
        [tr [td 'text(elem)'] [td '1'] [td 'Body text of elem as string; concatenates text children']]]
      [body
        """The wider string toolkit — trim, split/join, replace,
           case-folding variants, padding — is the
           `cx-stdlib/strings` module (`[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']`,
           then `[$strings:trim …]`, `[$strings:split …]`,
           `[$strings:join …]`, `[$strings:replace …]`). Regular
           expressions are `cx-stdlib/re`. See the Standard-library pages."""]
      [fixture id=program-builtin-g6-24-string-builtins]]

    [child id=numeric-builtins n=6.7.3 title='Numeric built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on numeric scalars. `sum`/`min`/`max`/`avg`
           operate on a sequence and skip non-numeric items;
           `floor`/`ceiling`/`round`/`abs` operate on a single
           value. A numeric operand is an `int`, a `float`, or one
           of the exact kinds `decimal` / `bigint` — and the exact
           kinds are their own family, not floats in disguise: a
           decimal or bigint operand folds exactly (`int` embeds
           losslessly), while mixing one with a float raises
           CXER0100 — `[cast]` is the only bridge. That is the
           arithmetic heads' rule; the aggregates are a different
           operand shape, never a different numeric semantics."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'builtin'] [th 'arity'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'sum(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Sum of numeric scalars; empty yields int 0; int/float mix → float; decimal/bigint fold exactly']]
        [tr [td 'min(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Minimum numeric, in its own kind; empty yields int 0']]
        [tr [td 'max(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Maximum numeric, in its own kind; empty yields int 0']]
        [tr [td 'avg(seq)'] [td '1'] [td 'Arithmetic mean — float, or exact over decimal/bigint; empty yields float 0.0']]
        [tr [td 'abs(x)'] [td '1'] [td 'Absolute value; preserves int/float kind']]
        [tr [td 'floor(x)'] [td '1'] [td 'Largest integer ≤ x; int passes through']]
        [tr [td 'ceiling(x)'] [td '1'] [td 'Smallest integer ≥ x; int passes through']]
        [tr [td 'round(x)'] [td '1'] [td 'Half-away-from-zero rounding (XPath fn:round)']]
        [tr [td 'mod(a, b)'] [td '2'] [td 'Remainder; sign follows dividend; b=0 raises CXER0101']]
        [tr [td 'div(a, b)'] [td '2'] [td 'True division if either is float; integer division if both int; exact over decimal/bigint, as `/`']]
        [tr [td 'idiv(a, b)'] [td '2'] [td 'Integer (truncating) division regardless of operand kinds; exact quotient over decimal/bigint']]]
      [body
        """Exact division either terminates or says so: a
           decimal/bigint quotient that does not terminate raises
           CXER3002 and the refusal names `$math:div-decimal`, the
           verb carrying an explicit precision + mode. There is no
           default rounding context. `/`, `div` and an exact `avg`
           are one implementation, so they never disagree. `idiv`
           is exempt — an integral quotient always terminates."""]
      [body
        """Type coercion is the single explicit `cast` operator —
           `[cast '42' :int]`, `[cast 3 :float]`, `[cast 42
           :string]` — there is no `to-int` / `to-float` family; a
           failed cast is `CXER0290` (E_CAST_FAILED). CX floats are
           IEEE-754: `NaN` and `±Inf` exist and follow IEEE-754
           semantics — test for NaN with `[$math:is-nan]` from
           `cx-stdlib/math`. `CXER0101` (E_ARITH_DIVIDE_ZERO) is
           division or modulo by zero, specifically. Exponentiation
           is `[$math:pow]` in `cx-stdlib/math`."""]
      [fixture id=program-builtin-g6-25-numeric-builtins]]

    [child id=logical-builtins n=6.7.4 title='Logical built-ins'
      [body
        """Boolean combinators and EBV-aware tests. `and` / `or`
           short-circuit; `not` is total over any EBV-typed
           argument."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'builtin'] [th 'arity'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'not(v)'] [td '1'] [td 'Boolean negation under EBV (cxdm §6); an Iterator operand raises — force it first']]
        [tr [td 'and(a, b, …)'] [td '≥1'] [td 'Boolean — true iff all args truthy; short-circuit L→R; bare operator head']]
        [tr [td 'or(a, b, …)'] [td '≥1'] [td 'Boolean — true iff any arg truthy; short-circuit L→R; bare operator head']]
        [tr [td '= (a, b)'] [td '2'] [td 'Structural equality as an operator head — types, values, attributes, children all match']]]
      [body
        """`and` / `or` / `not` are bare operator heads
           (`[and P Q]`, no `$` sigil), and so is `=`: over elements it
           is structural equality, the same comparison the value
           operators use. There is no `xor` / `if()` / `instance-of`
           builtin — branch with `[?if]`, and test types by schema
           validation or `[?match]` shape dispatch."""]
      [fixture id=program-builtin-g6-26-logical-builtins]]

    [child id=accessor-builtins n=6.7.5 title='Node-accessor built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on an Element node — read structural
           properties. Script-level equivalents of the Layer-1
           binding methods (see [[layer-1]])."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'builtin'] [th 'arity'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'name(elem)'] [td '1'] [td 'Element name as a string (including any namespace prefix); non-element raises CXER0100']]
        [tr [td 'local-name(elem)'] [td '1'] [td 'Element name without namespace prefix']]
        [tr [td 'string(value)'] [td '1'] [td "A scalar's canonical text, or an element/attribute's text content"]]
        [tr [td 'text(elem)'] [td '1'] [td 'Body text of elem; concatenates text children (string builtin, listed for discoverability)']]]
      [body
        """Structural navigation is CXPath, not accessor calls:
           children are `$e/*`, the parent is
           `//x/parent::*` (document-anchored), attributes are
           `$e@name`. Inside a predicate the reserved bindings
           `$_position` and `$_last` replace XPath's
           `position()` / `last()` — there are no zero-arity
           accessor calls."""]
      [fixture id=program-builtin-g6-27-node-accessors]]]

  [child id=errors n=6.8 title='Errors as values'
    [intro
      """CX treats errors as ordinary values. An error is a CX
         element with kind `[err :code "cx-err:CXERnnnn" …]`. It
         can be passed around, pattern-matched, transformed, and
         recovered from. Full error registry in
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §9.5."""]

    [body
      """**Propagation operators.** Two postfix operators thread
         errors through expressions:"""]
    [list
      [item """`f($x)?` — call `f`; if it errored, propagate
              the error unchanged through the surrounding
              directive (which catches it). This is the "early
              return" form."""]
      [item """`f($x)!` — call `f`; if it errored, raise
              immediately as a host exception (cross the
              CX/binding boundary). Use sparingly — it converts
              the error from a value to a runtime trap."""]]

    [body
      """A lookup that fails on one item recovers by matching the
         `[err]` it produced, and the loop goes on:"""]
    [fixture id=program-err-003-on-error-recovery]

    [body
      """Recovery is `[?match]` (see [[match]]) over the `[err]`
         channel: the errorable expression is the scrutinee, and an
         `[err]` it produces is captured (not propagated) so a
         `[case [err …] …]` arm can dispatch on its code. Match by
         plain attribute equality (`[err code='CXER0204']`) or capture
         the code (`[err @code=$c]`). Arms evaluate first-match-wins; an
         `[else …]` (or bare `$err` capture) is the catch-all. With no
         catch-all and no match, the `[err]` propagates to the next
         enclosing `[?match]` or out of the program. For a plain
         value-or-default, `[?else EXPR DEFAULT]` (§8.13) is the
         lighter sugar; `[?fallback PRIMARY [recover-with …]]` (§10.2.4)
         binds `$err` in the recovery."""]

    [body
      """**Why not exceptions.** Exceptions contaminate every signature
         with an invisible \"may throw\" that no tool can see, and force
         every caller to either catch or be transparent. In CX the
         error is in the value, the propagation discipline is in the
         operators (`?` and `!`), and the handling is in `[?match]` —
         all three visible in the source. The cost of an error path is
         the cost of one value: no stack scan, no unwind tables. And
         because an error is just a value, it crosses every boundary
         values cross — pipelines, `[?await]`, worker channels — without
         a parallel control-flow mechanism: a worker that errors sends
         the `[err]` through its result channel and terminates
         cleanly."""]

    [child id=error-registry n=6.8.1 title='Error code registry (CXER0100–0299)'
      [body
        """The CXER0100–0299 range is reserved for code-language
           errors. Errors are organized into subsystems by tens
           digits. The complete normative map is in
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §9.5 — this
           subsection is the developer-facing summary."""]

      [body
        """**Parse / arithmetic / application — CXER010x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0100'] [td 'PARSE_ERROR'] [td 'Pattern syntax invalid; directive name not in registry; arity mismatch'] [td 'Fix source — this is author error, not runtime']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0101'] [td 'E_ARITH_DIVIDE_ZERO'] [td 'Division or modulo by zero — [$div] / [$mod] / [$idiv] and the / and % operator heads'] [td 'Guard the divisor, or [?match] [case [err code="cx-err:CXER0101"] …]']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0102'] [td 'E_PARTIAL_APP'] [td 'Invalid partial application — a _ hole in a rest position, or a partial applied with more arguments than held positions'] [td 'Author error — fix the call shape']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0103'] [td 'MULTI_VALUED_PREDICATE'] [td 'Value-comparison or predicate with a multi-valued operand'] [td 'Filter the sequence and compare single values ([$exists //x[= $_ V]])']]]
      [body
        """Nearby classes that are **not** CXER010x: anchor / merge
           errors are data-language `E` codes
           ([`spec/core/cxdm.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §11);
           a sized-integer literal out of range is the lex-time
           `CXERLEX-RANGE` refusal; a failed `cast` is `CXER0290`
           (E_CAST_FAILED), recovered by matching on the code or
           spelling a representable value — coercion is always the
           explicit `[cast V :int]` form."""]

      [body
        """**Resilience — CXER014x / CXER015x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0140'] [td 'RETRY_EXHAUSTED'] [td '[?retry] reached max= attempts without success'] [td '[?match] [case [err code="CXER0140"] …]; tune max= / backoff=']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0141'] [td 'TIMEOUT'] [td '[?timeout] DURATION elapsed'] [td '[on-timeout EXPR] or [?match] [case [err code="CXER0141"] …]']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0150'] [td 'BREAKER_OPEN'] [td '[?circuit-breaker] tripped; rejecting calls'] [td 'Wait :reset DURATION; fallback path']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0151'] [td 'RATE_LIMITED'] [td '[?rate-limit] tokens exhausted'] [td 'Honor :retry-after slot in the err; back off']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0152'] [td 'BULKHEAD_FULL'] [td '[?bulkhead] max-concurrent= + queue= exhausted'] [td 'Backpressure caller; reduce concurrency']]]

      [body
        """**Services — CXER016x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0160'] [td 'BAD_REQUEST'] [td 'HTTP 400 — malformed body or path-param parse failure'] [td 'Return [response status=400 [body $err]] to client']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0161'] [td 'UNAUTHORIZED'] [td 'HTTP 401 — :auth returned err'] [td 'Return 401; client re-authenticates']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0162'] [td 'NOT_FOUND'] [td 'HTTP 404 — no resource matched path + method'] [td 'Return 404; client checks URL']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0163'] [td 'REQUEST_TIMEOUT'] [td 'HTTP 408 — :read-timeout exceeded'] [td 'Retry with backoff; reduce request size']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0164'] [td 'PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE'] [td 'HTTP 413 — body > max-body-bytes='] [td 'Chunk upload; increase max-body-bytes= if appropriate']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0165'] [td 'INTERNAL_ERROR'] [td 'HTTP 500 — unhandled err in resource body'] [td 'Recover the body with [?match]/[?fallback]; log and return generic 500 to client']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0166'] [td 'SHUTTING_DOWN'] [td 'HTTP 503 — service in shutdown drain'] [td 'Client retries elsewhere; do not retry same instance']]]

      [body
        """**Client — CXER018x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0180'] [td 'CONNECTION_REFUSED'] [td 'TCP connect failure'] [td 'Retry with backoff; check :target URL']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0181'] [td 'TLS_HANDSHAKE_FAILED'] [td 'TLS negotiation failed'] [td 'Check :tls config; cert validity; retry']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0182'] [td 'INVALID_RESPONSE'] [td 'Server response could not be parsed'] [td 'Log raw response; report upstream issue']]]

      [body
        """**Concurrency — CXER020x / CXER022x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0200'] [td 'CHANNEL_CLOSED'] [td 'Receive from drained-closed channel; send to closed channel'] [td 'Treat as normal stream-end; [?match] on err code']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0201'] [td 'SEND_TIMEOUT'] [td '[?try-send] buffer-full timeout'] [td 'Drop, queue elsewhere, or back off']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0202'] [td 'RECV_TIMEOUT'] [td '[?try-receive] empty timeout'] [td 'Poll again; abandon work if pattern persists']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0203'] [td 'CHANNEL_ALREADY_CLOSED'] [td 'Second [?close] on same channel'] [td 'Idempotent close idiom — [?else [?close $ch] ()]']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0220'] [td 'WORKER_PANIC'] [td 'Worker body raised unhandled err'] [td 'Inspect cause= attribute; restart worker or supervisor pattern']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0221'] [td 'WORKER_CANCELLED'] [td 'Worker terminated via [?cancel]'] [td 'Expected on graceful shutdown; clean up resources']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0222'] [td 'WORKER_NOT_FOUND'] [td '[?worker-handle name=N] lookup miss'] [td 'Recover with [?match] on the err code, or [?else] a default']]]

      [body
        """**Async — CXER024x / CXER026x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0240'] [td 'AWAIT_ALL_FAILED'] [td '[?await-all] saw ≥ 1 non-done future'] [td 'Inspect :causes slot — sequence of [err …] per failed future']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0241'] [td 'AWAIT_TIMEOUT'] [td '[?await $f timeout=DURATION] deadline expired (future itself still running)'] [td 'Tune timeout=; abandon or [?cancel $f]']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0260'] [td 'CANCELLED'] [td 'Operation observed cooperative cancellation'] [td 'Expected; clean up; do not retry']]]

      [body
        """**Visualization — CXER028x:**"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'symbolic'] [th 'meaning'] [th 'recovery']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0281'] [td 'UNRENDERABLE_DIRECTIVE'] [td 'Directive shape outside §10.1.2 locked render rules — the sole live renderer code (CXER0280 is retired to reserved)'] [td 'Adjust source; renderer is conservative on purpose']]]

      [body
        """The CXER0000–0099 range is reserved for data-language
           errors (parse, validate, canonicalize); see
           [[limits]] in the data section. CXER0300+ is
           reserved for future subsystems."""]]]

  [child id=concurrency n=6.9 title='Concurrency'
    [intro
      """CX has three concurrency surfaces, all spelled as
         directives. They build on the pure-functional core:
         every channel send, every worker call, every async
         await sees only immutable values, so there is no
         shared-mutable-state hazard. Spec:
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §10.3–§10.5."""]

    [child id=services n=6.9.1 title='Services and clients'
      [body
        """`[?http-service]` declares a named HTTP service with
           resource handlers. `[?http-client]` is its client
           side. Resources handle requests and return
           responses; the bodies are ordinary CX code."""]

      [body
        """**Service definition:**"""]
      [fixture id=program-svc-001-get-happy-path]
      [body
        """The service above is declared on `port=0` (the harness picks a
           free port) and the client call rides through the corpus's own
           `[?test-service-client]` helper, which binds `$test-target` to
           the running service; the tunables — `read-timeout=`,
           `write-timeout=`, `max-body-bytes=`, `max-connections=`,
           `grace-period=` — are attributes on the same element."""]

      [body
        """**Resource parameters.** Each `[resource]` declares
           `method=METHOD path=PATH`, optional `produces=` /
           `consumes=` MIME attributes, optional `[auth …]`
           predicate-expression clause, and a body expression
           in positional position. PATH supports `:name`
           parameter binding — `/users/:id` exposes
           `$request/path-params/id` inside the body."""]

      [body
        """**Request and response shapes:**"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-28-request-and-response-shapes]

      [body
        """**Lifecycle.** `[?http-service]` returns a handle.
           `[?service-handle name=N]` looks up an existing
           service. `[?stop $handle]` initiates graceful
           shutdown — drains in-flight requests for up to
           `grace-period=`, then force-closes; new requests
           during drain receive HTTP 503 (`CXER0166`).
           Process SIGTERM / SIGINT also triggers graceful
           shutdown."""]

      [body
        """**HTTP client.** `[?http-client target=URL …]`
           returns a client value; operations pipe through
           it:"""]
      [fixture id=program-svc-002-post-happy-path]
      [body
        """A client takes `timeout=` and a `[resilience …]` child (any of
           the [[resilience]] directives) on the same element as
           `target=`."""]

      [body
        """**Status.** Services are a stable surface;
           Tier-1 V binding ships the full implementation.
           Tier-2 bindings expose `[?http-client]` and
           `[?service-handle]` lookup but service-hosting may
           defer to the host's native HTTP framework. See
           [[bindings]]."""]]

    [child id=workers n=6.9.2 title='Workers and channels'
      [body
        """`[?worker]` spawns a concurrent worker. `[?channel]`
           creates a FIFO message queue. `[?send]` and
           `[?receive]` move values across. `[?select]` is the
           multi-way receive."""]

      [fixture id=program-conc-g6-29-channel-send-receive]
      [body
        """A producer worker and a consumer over one channel keep the
           producer's order:"""]
      [fixture id=program-conc-008-fifo-single-producer]

      [body
        """**Channel semantics.** `[?channel name=… buffer=N]` has
           a bounded FIFO buffer. `buffer=0` is synchronous
           (rendezvous — send blocks until receive). FIFO
           ordering is preserved per producer-consumer pair;
           with multiple producers, per-producer FIFO is
           preserved but there is no global cross-producer
           ordering."""]

      [body
        """**Send / receive variants.**"""]
      [list
        [item """`[?send V to=CH]` — block when buffer full;
                returns `[ok]` once buffered. Returns
                `CXER0200` (CHANNEL_CLOSED) if CH is
                closed."""]
        [item """`[?try-send V to=CH timeout=DURATION]` —
                same but returns `CXER0201` (SEND_TIMEOUT) on
                buffer-full timeout."""]
        [item """`[?receive from=CH]` — block when empty;
                returns next value. Returns `CXER0200` when CH
                is closed and drained."""]
        [item """`[?try-receive from=CH timeout=DURATION]` —
                same but returns `CXER0202` (RECV_TIMEOUT) on
                empty-channel timeout."""]
        [item """`[?close CH]` — mark closed. Subsequent
                `[?send]` fails immediately; subsequent
                `[?receive]` drains buffered values then
                returns CHANNEL_CLOSED. Double-close raises
                `CXER0203`."""]]

      [body
        """**Worker observability.** Workers are
           handle-addressable:"""]
      [fixture id=program-conc-012-worker-cancel-after-done-noop]
      [body
        """`[?wait-for worker=$w]` blocks until the worker is done,
           `[?cancel worker=$w]` requests cooperative cancellation (a
           no-op once the worker has finished, as above), and
           `[?check-cancel]` is the polling point inside a hot loop (see
           [[async]])."""]

      [body
        """**Select — multi-way receive.** Evaluates all
           `[case …]` clauses concurrently; proceeds with the
           first ready. Uniform-random selection on
           simultaneous readiness:"""]
      [fixture id=program-conc-g6-30-select-first-ready]
      [note
        """**Shape.** `[?select]` clauses are clause-children:
           `[case [from CH $msg] HANDLER]` for a receive and
           `[case [timeout DURATION] HANDLER]` for a deadline. The
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
           §10.4.7 registry holds the authoritative shape. The v0.7.x `:case [:from CH $msg H]` [; version-literal-ok ]
           colon-slot form is retired — there is no dual-accept."""]]

    [child id=async n=6.9.3 title='Async, await, and cancellation'
      [body
        """`[?async EXPR]` evaluates EXPR in a new async
           context and returns a future immediately:"""]

      [fixture id=program-async-g6-31-await-all]

      [body
        """**Future state machine.** Futures progress through
           `pending → running → done` / `failed` /
           `cancelled`. Once terminal, the state is
           immutable."""]

      [body
        """**Barrier variants:**"""]
      [list
        [item """`[?await $f]` — block until terminal; return
                value on done, propagate err on failed,
                CANCELLED on cancelled."""]
        [item """`[?await $f timeout=DURATION]` — add a hard
                deadline; on expiry return `CXER0241`
                (AWAIT_TIMEOUT). The future continues running
                — `timeout=` bounds the caller's wait, not the
                future itself."""]
        [item """`[?await-all FUTURES]` — block until all
                terminal. If all done, yield sequence of
                values. If any failed/cancelled, yield
                `CXER0240` with a `[causes …]` child carrying
                every non-done cause in input order."""]
        [item """`[?await-any FUTURES]` — yield first to
                terminate. Other futures continue running;
                their results are discarded."""]
        [item """`[?await-race FUTURES]` — yield first to
                terminate; **other futures are cancelled**.
                Use for "I only need one"."""]]

      [body
        """**Cooperative cancellation contract.** `[?cancel
           $f]` requests cancellation — it is a signal, not
           a kill. The runtime guarantees cancellation
           observation at:"""]
      [list
        [item """HTTP client calls at next network read/write
                boundary."""]
        [item """`[?sleep DURATION]` — immediately."""]
        [item """`[?send]` / `[?receive]` / `[?try-send]` /
                `[?try-receive]` — immediately, returning
                `CXER0260` (CANCELLED)."""]
        [item """`[?for]` — at every iteration boundary."""]
        [item """`[?await]` — immediately."""]]

      [body
        """**Pure CPU loops without yield points do NOT
           observe cancellation.** Authors who want a
           cancellable hot loop must insert `[?check-cancel]`
           at appropriate boundaries — it evaluates to `[ok]`
           normally, or `CXER0260` (CANCELLED) if
           cancellation is pending."""]

      [fixture id=program-async-g6-32-check-cancel-in-a-loop]

      [body
        """**Composition with resilience.** `[?async]`
           composes with every resilience directive.
           `[?async [?timeout 5s [$slow-call]]]` returns
           a future that resolves to `CXER0141` (TIMEOUT)
           after 5s if `slow-call` hasn't completed."""]

      [body
        """**Status.** Async/await is a stable surface of the
           reference evaluator; binding parity ships per
           [`spec/misc/parity-matrix.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]]

  [child id=resilience n=6.10 title='Resilience directives'
    [intro
      """The six resilience directives wrap an inner
         expression with a failure-handling policy. They are
         not new control-flow primitives — they are normal CX
         code with cleanly-named policies. Spec:
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §10.2."""]

    [body
      """**Common error shape.** Every resilience directive
         on terminal failure emits a structured `[err …]`
         per the §10.2.7 error-code matrix (see
         [[error-registry]])."""]

    [child id=retry n=6.10.1 title='[?retry] — bounded re-run with backoff'
      [body
        """Re-run an inner body on `[err …]` return, up to
           `max=` attempts, sleeping between attempts per
           `backoff=` / `delay=` / `jitter=`. Terminal
           failure raises `CXER0140` (RETRY_EXHAUSTED)."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'max=INT'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Maximum attempts; must be > 0']]
        [tr [td 'backoff=STRATEGY'] [td 'exponential'] [td 'constant / linear / exponential / fibonacci']]
        [tr [td 'delay=DURATION'] [td '100ms'] [td 'Base delay; combined with backoff formula']]
        [tr [td 'jitter=MODE'] [td 'equal'] [td 'none / full / equal / decorrelated']]
        [tr [td '[on PREDICATE]'] [td '[?fn ($e) true]'] [td 'Per-err filter; retry iff truthy']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Inner expression to retry']]]
      [body
        """**Backoff formulas** (delay for attempt N, N ≥ 1):
           `constant` = `delay=`; `linear` = `delay × N`;
           `exponential` = `delay × 2^(N-1)`; `fibonacci` =
           `delay × fib(N)`."""]
      [body
        """**Jitter modes** (D = pre-jitter delay): `none` =
           `D`; `full` = `uniform(0, D)`; `equal` = `D/2 +
           uniform(0, D/2)`; `decorrelated` = `uniform(delay,
           last_delay × 3)`."""]
      [body
        """The full policy reads `[?retry max=5 backoff=exponential
           delay=200ms jitter=equal [on [?fn ($e) [= $e@code
           'CXER0163']]] BODY]` — retry only on a timeout. The fixture
           below retries a body that fails twice and succeeds on the
           third attempt:"""]
      [fixture id=program-retry-002-success-on-third-attempt]]

    [child id=timeout n=6.10.2 title='[?timeout] — hard deadline'
      [body
        """Bound execution time of an inner body. On expiry,
           cooperatively cancel the body and return
           the `[on-timeout …]` clause body (if present) or
           `[result status=err code='cx-err:CXER0141'
           elapsed=DURATION]`."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'DURATION (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Hard deadline; e.g. 5s, 250ms']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Inner expression with cooperative cancel points']]
        [tr [td '[on-timeout EXPR]'] [td '(none)'] [td 'Fallback expression on expiry; otherwise raises CXER0141']]]
      [fixture id=program-timeout-003-on-timeout-recovery]]

    [child id=circuit-breaker n=6.10.3 title='[?circuit-breaker] — fail-fast on consecutive failures'
      [body
        """Three states: **closed** (passes through), **open**
           (rejects with `CXER0150` (BREAKER_OPEN) without
           calling `:body`), **half-open** (allows one
           probe). Transitions:"""]
      [list
        [item """Closed → open when failure ratio in rolling
                `window=` exceeds `threshold=`, with at least
                `min-samples=` recorded."""]
        [item """Open → half-open after `reset=` elapses."""]
        [item """Half-open → closed if probe succeeds."""]
        [item """Half-open → open if probe fails."""]]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'threshold=RATIO'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Failure ratio 0.0–1.0 to trip breaker']]
        [tr [td 'window=DURATION'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Rolling-window length for sampling']]
        [tr [td 'reset=DURATION'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Half-open delay after trip']]
        [tr [td 'min-samples=INT'] [td '10'] [td 'Minimum sample count before threshold check']]
        [tr [td 'name=STR'] [td '(lexical position)'] [td 'State-identity key for cross-position sharing']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Inner expression']]]
      [fixture id=program-cb-002-trips-open]]

    [child id=fallback n=6.10.4 title='[?fallback] — alternative on error'
      [body
        """If primary returns `[err …]`, evaluate secondary
           and return its value (which may itself be `[err]`
           — `[?fallback]` does not wrap that case)."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'First expression tried (primary)']]
        [tr [td '[recover-with SECONDARY]'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Fallback expression evaluated only on primary err']]]
      [fixture id=program-fallback-002-primary-err-secondary-ok]
      [note
        """`$err` **in the recovery scope.** Inside the recovery
           scope, `$err` is bound to the primary's `[err …]` value, so
           recovery can introspect the failure (e.g. `[recover-with
           [response status=500 reason=$err/@message]]`). The
           clause-child `[recover-with …]` form is the sole accepted
           shape (no legacy colon-slot)."""]]

    [child id=rate-limit n=6.10.5 title='[?rate-limit] — token-bucket cap'
      [body
        """Permits at most `max=` invocations per `per=`
           window. Excess invocations return `CXER0151`
           (RATE_LIMITED) with a `retry-after=DURATION`
           attribute — the time until the next token frees."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'max=INT'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Tokens per window; must be > 0']]
        [tr [td 'per=DURATION'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Refill window']]
        [tr [td 'name=STR'] [td '(lexical position)'] [td 'State-identity key']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Inner expression']]]
      [fixture id=program-ratelimit-002-over-limit]]

    [child id=bulkhead n=6.10.6 title='[?bulkhead] — bound concurrent calls'
      [body
        """Bounds concurrent invocations. If `max-concurrent=`
           slots are busy and `queue=` slots are full,
           returns `CXER0152` (BULKHEAD_FULL). Queued
           requests wait FIFO for a free slot."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'parameter'] [th 'default'] [th 'semantics']]
        [tr [td 'max-concurrent=INT'] [td '(required)'] [td 'In-flight cap; must be > 0']]
        [tr [td 'queue=INT'] [td '0'] [td 'Wait-queue depth; 0 = no queue, fail-fast on full']]
        [tr [td 'name=STR'] [td '(lexical position)'] [td 'State-identity key']]
        [tr [td 'body (positional)'] [td '(required)'] [td 'Inner expression']]]
      [fixture id=program-bulkhead-002-saturated-no-queue]]

    [child id=resilience-composition n=6.10.7 title='Composition'
      [body
        """Resilience directives compose by nesting. The
           outermost runs first, so its policy sees the
           inner policies' err codes. Standard pattern reads
           top-down:"""]
      [fixture id=program-compose-001-retry-over-timeout]
      [body
        """Reading: retry up to 3 times a deadline-bounded body; the
           timeout's `CXER0141` is what `[?retry]` sees on each attempt,
           and the exhausted retry names it as the cause. A breaker in
           the same position surfaces `CXER0150`, and `[?retry]` decides
           whether to keep retrying (the default `[on …]` predicate
           returns truthy → yes; override to scope which errs retry)."""]
      [body
        """**State identity (stateful directives).**
           `[?circuit-breaker]`, `[?rate-limit]`, and
           `[?bulkhead]` carry state across invocations. By
           default state is keyed by lexical source-text
           position — every evaluation of the same source
           node (including iterated under `[?for]` or
           `[?map … [par]]`) shares state. Pass `name=STR`
           to key by name instead, enabling cross-position
           sharing (one rate-limit budget enforced by
           callers in different modules)."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/evaluation.cx and
     docs-src/content/reference/code.cx during Phase B docs → guide
     consolidation (2026-05-24). Higher-level framing of how
     directives, patterns, and comprehensions compose. #]

  [child id=evaluation-framing n=6.11 title='Evaluation framing — directives, patterns, comprehensions'
    [intro
      """The directive registry above lists each form; this section
         shows how the forms fit together — directives as the
         language layer over data, pattern matching as the way
         programs walk a tree, comprehensions as the composition
         glue. CXPath is a value kind (with `[?find]` retired) and
         `[expr]` is the general predicate."""]

    [child id=directives-overview n=6.11.1 title='Directives turn data into a language'
      [body
        """A directive is a CX element whose name starts with `?`.
           It carries semantics — iteration, branching,
           substitution, include, function call — that the
           evaluator interprets when running a CX program.
           Directives parse as regular CX, evaluate to text or
           values, and compose with each other through their slot
           bodies."""]
      [example lang=text
        """EXPR                                     [; bare expression evaluates ]
           [?for [in $x PATH] [yield BODY]]         [; iterate ]
           [?if COND [then THEN] [else ELSE]]       [; branch ]
           [?let [= $x EXPR] BODY]                  [; bind a name ]
           [?def name ($a $b) BODY]                 [; define a function ]"""]]

    [child id=patterns-walking n=6.11.2 title='Pattern matching — walking the tree'
      [body
        """The current program surface replaces XPath-style queries
           with `[?for]` pattern generators and CXPath path values
           (`[?find]` retired). A pattern is a CX
           literal where element names match by shape, attribute
           predicates restrict by value, and `$bindings` capture
           matched subtrees. `[?for]` walks the tree (deep by
           default) and binds for each match; `[?match]` tests a
           single value (multi-arm dispatch)."""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-33-pattern-generators-walk-the-tree]]

    [child id=comprehensions n=6.11.3 title='Comprehensions and composition'
      [body
        """`[?for]` iterates a sequence (or matches a pattern across
           the tree) and yields per-iteration values; `[where …]`
           filters, `[order-by …]` sorts, `[group-by …]` aggregates.
           `[?let]` binds names; `[?if]` branches. The `[?pipe]`
           directive composes them."""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-34-comprehension-let-pipe]

      [child id=comprehensions-future-surface n=6.11.3.1
             title='Future surface — ranges, slices, Iterator'
        [body
          """A future surface extends the
             comprehension surface with **strided ranges**
             (`[$range 1 99 2]`, `[$range 1 *]`), **slice expressions on a
             binding postfix** (`$xs[2:5]`, `$xs[::-1]`,
             `$xs[:, "name"]`), **first-class slice values** (`[?def
             $s [2:$_last:2]]`), and a new **`Iterator` value
             kind** in CXDM §2 alongside Sequence / Array / Map.
             Comprehensions become lazy by default; named iterators
             (`[?def]`) memoize on first walk so re-use is safe."""]

        [body
          """Four worked examples (§W1–§W4) each have a paired
             conformance fixture
             `program-slice-wN-*` in
             [`conformance/code.cxd`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/conformance/code.cxd);
             until the implementation lands the fixtures are
             `out_err: cx-err:CXER0100` (parse-deferred), then flip
             to `out_text` in a single commit per fixture. The
             reconciliation test
             `program-cxpath-fncall-slice-composition`
             verifies grammar disambiguation against the fused
             predicate-body closure
             ([`spec/formal/grammar.ebnf`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf)
             [159b]) — bare-fn-call, path predicate, and slice
             postfix coexist."""]

        [body
          """The W1 program — a strided range, a slice, a comprehension —
             is pinned today at its parse refusal, and flips to its
             output the day the surface lands:"""]
        [fixture id=program-slice-w1-strided-range-then-slice]

        [body
          """The path-to-iterator taxonomy (D26): a CXPath value is
             **not** an `Iterator` — it is a *description of a
             selection*. Evaluation materialises it to a `sequence`
             (eager, the kind currently in use). The `sequence`
             flows into Iterator combinators on opt-in via `[?for]`;
             walking an Iterator back to a concrete collection is the
             registered force-materializer family `[?to-sequence]` /
             `[?to-array]` / `[?to-map]` (a `[?to-iterator]`
             directive is planned but not registered). There is no
             `iterator → path` direction — paths describe; iterators
             walk."""]]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/reference/{cxpath,errors}.cx during
     Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). CXPath
     reference complements §6.3; errors complements §6.8. #]

  [child id=cxpath-quickref n=6.12 title='CXPath reference — quick form'
    [intro
      """At-a-glance CXPath reference. The normative grammar lives
         in [`spec/formal/grammar.ebnf`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf)
         productions [130]/[131]/[135] (path steps / binding paths) and
         [159]/[160a] (general PredicateExpr + the `(bind $name)`
         step annotation). XPath 3.1 alignment and divergences are
         in [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md) §5.5.
         CXPath is a first-class value kind;
         general predicates with `$_` / `$_position` / `$_last`."""]

    [child id=cxpath-quick-table n=6.12.1 title='Quick reference'
      [body
        """In order: every user; every active user; the active users'
           emails; the third user in document order; everything two
           levels under the root; the siblings after the first user; the
           ancestors of a `[para]`; a general predicate — prefix code
           over `$_`; a range; and the three set heads `union`,
           `intersect`, `except`:"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-35-quick-reference]]

    [child id=cxpath-four-forms n=6.12.2 title='Four leading forms'
      [body
        """Every CXPath has one of four leading-token forms.
           - `//` **descendant** — `//user` —
             `descendant-or-self::node()/child::user`; the most
             common shape.
           - `/` **absolute** — `/root/item` — child of the
             document root; rare in well-shaped data.
           - **relative** — `user/email` — applied against the
             current evaluation context.
           - `$name/` **binding** — `$u/email` — rooted at a
             previously-bound value (per `[?for]` / `[?let]` /
             `(bind $name)`)."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-axes n=6.12.3 title='Twelve axes'
      [body
        """Every step can specify an axis. The default axis is
           `child::`; the `attribute::` axis has the dedicated
           `@` sigil. CX implements all twelve
           XPath 3.1 axes: `child`, `descendant`,
           `descendant-or-self`, `parent`, `ancestor`,
           `ancestor-or-self`, `self`, `following-sibling`,
           `preceding-sibling`, `following`, `preceding`,
           `attribute`."""]
      [body
        """Composite shortcut forms: `/name` ≡ `child::name`;
           `//name` ≡ `descendant-or-self::node()/child::name`;
           `@name` ≡ `attribute::name`; `..` ≡ `parent::node()`;
           `.` ≡ `self::node()`. Attribute axis is name-disjoint
           from element axes — `//user/@name` selects the
           attribute only; `//user/name` selects the child
           element only."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-predicates n=6.12.4 title='General predicate + reserved bindings'
      [body
        """Predicates filter the step sequence. A predicate body
           is prefix CX code coerced to a boolean via the
           Effective Boolean Value (EBV) rule. The atomic
           shorthands — `[@name]` / `[@!name]` (attribute
           present / absent), `[N]` (position), a bare child name
           (step-existence) — remain available and round-trip via
           canonical re-emit. Value comparisons are always prefix:
           `[= $_@name V]`."""]
      [body
        """**Three reserved bindings** in scope inside every
           predicate body: `$_` (current candidate item, type
           matches the candidate kind), `$_position` (1-based int
           index — equivalent to XPath `position()`), `$_last`
           (int cardinality of the candidate sequence —
           equivalent to XPath `last()`). Predicates are eagerly
           materialised, so `$_last` is known on
           every predicate evaluation."""]
      [body
        """**Comparison sigils** are value-comparison only: `=`,
           `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`. No keyword synonyms (no
           `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/etc.). Comparing a sequence with more
           than one item raises `CXER0103`. **Purity** — every
           predicate body MUST be pure; impure
           callees fail load with `CXER0230`."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-bind n=6.12.5 title='Cross-step reference — nested generators'
      [body
        """To reference an **outer** step's focus from an inner
           predicate ("leads of teams with at least three
           members"), stack `[?for]` generators — each generator
           names its focus, and every later clause sees the
           earlier bindings:"""]
      [fixture id=program-for-g6-36-nested-generators-see-outer-bindings]
      [note
        """The grammar reserves a `(bind $name)` path-step
           annotation (grammar.ebnf [160a]) for naming a step's
           focus inline; the nested-generator form above is the
           conformance-exercised idiom."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-binding-surface n=6.12.6 title='In the host bindings'
      [body
        """Every binding exposes CXPath identically through its
           Layer-1 `Doc.select_all` — the same selector string, the
           same bytes returned across the C ABI; the bindings page shows
           each spelling. The selector, run here by `cx` itself:"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g6-37-active-emails-one-selector]]]

  [child id=errors-catalog n=6.13 title='Error code catalog'
    [intro
      """Every CX error carries a structured payload: `cx-err:CODE`
         then a unit separator (US, `0x1F`) then a human-readable
         description then optionally another unit separator and a
         value. Bindings preserve this shape so `[?match]` can match
         on code rather than on text. The catalog below
         covers the current ranges; the normative registry is
         [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         §9.4/§9.5, with the governance rules for code allocation in
         [`spec/process/governance.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
         §9.6."""]

    [child id=errors-security n=6.13.1 title='Capability and runtime caps'
      [body
        """- `CXER0271` — capability denied at an effect point.
             Capabilities are deny-by-default; the param-less eval
             entry points run with the **empty capability set** —
             "pure-only" is the empty set, not a pragma.
           - `CXER0272` — `E_STACK_EXHAUSTED`: the evaluator's
             native-stack exhaustion guard."""]]

    [child id=errors-eval-gates n=6.13.2 title='Purity and eval gates'
      [body
        """- `CXER0230` — purity gate: an impure callee where
             purity is required (e.g. inside a CXPath predicate)
             is rejected at parse / module-load.
           - `CXER0233` — a `pure` def reaching `[?eval]` /
             `cx:eval-tree` (tree-eval is `impure`, like `cx:eval`).
           - `CXER4113` — lib-widen gate: `[?lib]` inside an eval
             tree may not widen the capability set.
           - `CXER4114` — eval depth gate: `cx:eval` / `[?eval]`
             recursion exceeded the shared depth budget."""]]

    [child id=errors-include n=6.13.3 title='E9xx — include resolver'
      [body
        """- `E901` — absolute path rejected.
           - `E902` — path escapes include root (lexical or
             symlink).
           - `E903` — URL-scheme path rejected.
           - `E904` — cycle detected.
           - `E905` — max depth exceeded.
           - `E906` — file not found.
           - `E907` — file not readable.
           - `E908` — path is a directory.
           - `E909` — I/O error during read.
           - `E910` — non-UTF-8 content.
           - `E911` — included file parse failure."""]]

    [child id=errors-catching n=6.13.4 title='Recovering with [?match]'
      [body
        """An ungranted read answers `CXER0271` — the capability
           refusal, an err value like any other:"""]
      [fixture id=io-001-read-file-cap-denied]
      [body
        """Under `--allow-read`, a read of a missing file answers
           `CXER3401`; either way a `[case [err @code=$c] …]` arm
           dispatches on the code by name, and the catch-all arms take
           whatever is left:"""]
      [fixture id=program-err-g6-38-recover-by-code]]]]
